    {
      "type": "generalise",
      "difficulty": 2,
      "prose": "Each time you took two points and divided the rise by the run. For points $(x_1, y_1)$ and $(x_2, y_2)$ on a line, the gradient $m$ is $$m = \\frac{\\text{rise}}{\\text{run}} = \\frac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1}$$",
      "question": "Use it for the points $(2, 7)$ and $(5, 1)$. What is $m$?",
      "widget": {
        "kind": "choice",
        "layout": "grid",
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "right",
            "text": "$-2$",
            "correct": true
          },
          {
            "id": "sign",
            "text": "$2$",
            "why": "You dropped the sign. $y$ goes from 7 down to 1, so the rise is $1 - 7 = -6$."
          },
          {
            "id": "swapped",
            "text": "$-\\tfrac{1}{2}$",
            "why": "You put the run on top. The rise goes on top: $-6 \\div 3$."
          }
        ]
      },
      "hints": [
        {
          "rung": "nudge",
          "text": "Work out $y_2 - y_1$ and $x_2 - x_1$ separately."
        },
        {
          "rung": "step",
          "text": "$\\dfrac{1 - 7}{5 - 2} = \\dfrac{-6}{3} = -2$."
        }
      ],
      "feedback": {
        "correct": "$\\dfrac{1 - 7}{5 - 2} = -2$.",
        "generic": "Not quite right. Put the y difference on top and the x difference underneath."
      },
      "explain": {
        "text": "The rise is $1 - 7 = -6$ and the run is $5 - 2 = 3$. So $m = -6 \\div 3 = -2$: the line falls 2 for each 1 across."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "derive",
      "difficulty": 3,
      "prose": "Does it matter which point we call the first? Measure from $(x_2, y_2)$ back to $(x_1, y_1)$ instead, and simplify.",
      "question": "Put the lines in order.",
      "widget": {
        "kind": "order",
        "lines": [
          {
            "id": "d1",
            "text": "$m = \\dfrac{y_1 - y_2}{x_1 - x_2}$, rise over run measured from the second point back to the first"
          },
          {
            "id": "d2",
            "text": "$m = \\dfrac{-(y_2 - y_1)}{-(x_2 - x_1)}$, each backward difference is minus the forward one"
          },
          {
            "id": "d3",
            "text": "$m = \\dfrac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1}$, the two minus signs cancel"
          },
          {
            "id": "d4",
            "text": "So either order gives the same gradient, as long as the top and bottom go the same way"
          }
        ],
        "answer": [
          "d1",
          "d2",
          "d3",
          "d4"
        ]
      },
      "hints": [
        {
          "rung": "nudge",
          "text": "Start with the gradient measured backwards."
        },
        {
          "rung": "step",
          "text": "Write each backward difference as minus a forward one, then cancel the two minus signs."
        }
      ],
      "feedback": {
        "correct": "Both differences flip together, so the fraction does not change.",
        "generic": "Not quite right. Start from the backward gradient and simplify it one step at a time."
      },
      "explain": {
        "text": "Measuring backwards flips the sign of the rise and of the run. Two sign flips cancel in a fraction. Flipping only one of them is what breaks the gradient."
      }
    },
